22:04:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Climate, work conditions, and diet can affect odor in any population. Hot and humid weather can increase sweating; certain foods can affect breath or sweat; and cooking odors can cling to hair and clothing. Research may discuss those factors at a population or regional level. But that still does not establish that Bengali people—or South Asians generally—have an inherent or reliably stronger body odor. “More sweating in hot weather” is not the same as “an ethnic group smells bad,” especially once people live and work in different climates, have different diets, hygiene routines, jobs, health conditions, and access to laundry or deodorant. Also, body odor is mainly produced when sweat interacts with skin bacteria. The biggest practical factors are individual: sweating level, clothing, laundering, antiperspirant use, diet, medication, and medical conditions. Some genetic differences affect body-odor compounds across populations, but they do not justify broad claims about a specific nationality or ethnic group. If you have the article, it is worth checking what it actually measured: sweat production? odor compounds? food odors? a particular region and climate? self-reported hygiene? Those are often much narrower claims than “these people have stronger body odor.” For real-life interactions, stick to the concrete issue: a particular person, clothing, food odor, or workspace—not their ethnicity.
